>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Evidence-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder for Acne</Fragment>
      The whey and acne link rests on a few small case series, not controlled trials. If you suspect your shake, here is what the evidence really says, how to test it properly, and the dairy-free powders that make the test easy.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#research">What the Research Says</a>
      <a href="#hypotheses">IGF-1, Insulin and Dairy</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#isolate">Isolate vs Concentrate</a>
      <a href="#alternatives">Plant, Egg and Beef</a>
      <a href="#label-extras">Sugar and Label Extras</a>
      <a href="#elimination">Running an Elimination Trial</a>
      <a href="#other-triggers">Other Triggers to Rule Out</a>
      <a href="#dermatologist">When to See a Dermatologist</a>
      <a href="#how-we-chose">How We Chose</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Search for protein powder and acne and you will find confident answers in both directions: whey wrecks your skin, or the link is a myth. The truth is less dramatic. A handful of dermatologists have reported patients whose acne appeared after starting whey and cleared after stopping it, and there is a plausible hormonal explanation involving insulin and IGF-1. But no controlled trial has tested it, and most people who use whey never notice a problem. If your skin got worse after you started a shake, the sensible response is a careful, time-limited switch to a non-dairy protein, not a panic. Below we go through what the research actually shows, whether whey isolate is any different, how to run a fair elimination trial, what else to rule out, and six label-checked powders, mostly pea, egg and beef, that make the experiment straightforward. This is not medical advice, and persistent or severe acne belongs with a dermatologist.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="research" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the Research Actually Says About Whey and Acne
          </h2>

          <p>
            The idea that whey protein causes breakouts did not come from nowhere. It started with dermatologists noticing a pattern in their own clinics and writing it up. In 2012 a report in the journal Cutis described five male athletes aged 14 to 18 who developed moderate to severe acne after starting whey supplements; in four of them the acne cleared after they stopped the whey. The same year, a report in Dermatology described five adult bodybuilders with facial or truncal acne that appeared after they began using whey protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            A small Brazilian study published in 2013 followed 30 people for 60 days after they started protein supplements at a gym, 22 of them on whey, and recorded a progressive rise in acne lesions over that period. A 2017 report described six adolescents who developed acne limited to the trunk after whey use. That is roughly the full body of whey-specific evidence: a handful of case reports and one small observational follow-up, with no control group in any of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because case reports cannot establish cause. Teenagers who start lifting are also at the age when acne peaks, are sweating more under tight clothing, may be eating more overall and are sometimes taking other supplements at the same time. Without a comparison group taking a different protein, there is no way to separate the whey from everything else that changed. Case series are useful for raising a question, and that is exactly what these did. They do not answer it.
          </p>

          <p>
            No randomized controlled trial has tested whey protein against a non-dairy protein with acne as the outcome, as far as we could find. Broader reviews of diet and acne, including a 2021 review in the International Journal of Dermatology and a 2022 systematic review in JAAD International, conclude that the evidence for dairy is mixed and that the stronger trial evidence relates to high glycemic load diets rather than to protein supplements specifically.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest summary is this: whey has been linked to acne in a small number of people, the signal is plausible but weak, and most people who use whey do not report breakouts. If your skin worsened after you started a whey shake, that is worth taking seriously for you. It is not proof that whey is bad for skin in general, and it is not a reason for someone with clear skin to avoid it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">EVIDENCE STRENGTH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Case reports, not trials.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The whey-acne link rests on case series of five or six patients and one 30-person observational follow-up. No controlled trial has tested it. Treat it as a reason to experiment on yourself, not as settled science.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hypotheses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Dairy Might Matter: IGF-1, Insulin and the Hypotheses
          </h2>

          <p>
            The wider dairy literature is bigger but still observational. A 2018 meta-analysis in Nutrients pooled 14 studies covering 78,529 people aged 7 to 30 and found that any dairy intake was associated with modestly higher odds of acne, an odds ratio of about 1.25, with milk at about 1.28. The authors flagged publication bias and large differences between studies, and asked readers to interpret the results with caution. A separate genetic study by the same group in more than 20,000 Danish adults found no link between milk intake and adult acne.
          </p>

          <p>
            The proposed mechanism centres on two hormones. Milk proteins, and whey in particular, cause a sharp insulin response for the amount of carbohydrate they contain. Dairy intake is also associated with higher circulating levels of insulin-like growth factor 1, or IGF-1. Both insulin and IGF-1 can stimulate the oil glands and the skin cells that line hair follicles, and both feed into a cell-growth signalling pathway called mTORC1 that some researchers have argued drives acne.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is a coherent hypothesis, and it explains why whey, the most insulin-provoking part of milk, would be the obvious suspect. But it is a hypothesis built from mechanism studies and associations, not a demonstrated chain of cause and effect in people taking protein shakes. Exercise itself also changes insulin sensitivity, and resistance training raises IGF-1 regardless of what you drink afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional bodies have stayed cautious. The American Academy of Dermatology's 2024 acne guidelines did not make a dietary recommendation and listed diet among the areas where better research is needed. The earlier version of the guidelines noted emerging data on high glycemic index diets and limited evidence on dairy, particularly skim milk, without telling patients to cut either.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a buyer, the practical point is narrow. If dairy proteins do play a role for some people, the likely culprits are the insulin and IGF-1 responses, and those come from milk protein itself rather than from the lactose or fat. That has a direct bearing on whether switching from concentrate to isolate is likely to help, which is the next question most people ask.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Protein Powders for Acne-Prone Skin
          </h2>

          <p>
            No protein powder has been shown to treat or prevent acne. These picks are chosen to make a clean dairy-free trial easy: a single clear protein source, a short ingredient list, low added sugar and, where available, third-party certification. Label figures are per the label or brand listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor; prices are approximate and were checked on the date in our label database. We have not tested these powders ourselves, and taste notes are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="isolate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Isolate vs Concentrate: Does It Make a Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey protein concentrate is typically 70 to 80 percent protein by weight, with the rest made up of lactose, milk fat and minerals. Whey isolate is filtered further, to roughly 90 percent protein or more, with only traces of lactose and fat. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate comparison</a> covers the processing in detail. For digestion, the difference is real: many people who are lactose intolerant tolerate isolate far better than concentrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            For acne, there is no good evidence either way. None of the case reports compared isolate with concentrate, and several did not specify which form the patients used. If the insulin and IGF-1 hypothesis is right, isolate would not be expected to help much, because it is still whey protein and still produces a strong insulin response gram for gram. Removing lactose and fat removes the parts least implicated by the hypothesis.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people do report that their skin settles after a switch to isolate, and that may be genuine for them. It may also reflect other changes that come with the switch, such as a shorter ingredient list, no added sugar, or a smaller serving. Those details are worth noticing, because they tell you more about what actually changed than the word isolate on the front of the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our view is that trying a clean, unflavored whey isolate is a reasonable first step only if you want to keep whey and your breakouts are mild. It is the smallest change. If your acne is moderate, persistent or scarring, or you have already tried isolate without improvement, the more informative test is to remove dairy protein entirely for a while and replace it with egg, beef or a plant protein. That is why most of our picks below are not whey at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            If digestion rather than skin is your main problem, our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder guide</a> covers both isolates and non-dairy options. And if you are deciding between dairy and plant protein on broader grounds, the trade-offs on amino acids, cost and taste are laid out in our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein comparison</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Plant, Egg and Beef: How the Alternatives Compare
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once dairy is out, there are three serious replacements. Pea protein is the most common plant option and the cheapest per gram. Egg white protein is a complete animal protein with an amino acid profile close to whey. Beef protein isolate is dairy-free and egg-free but tends to be built largely from connective tissue, which shifts its profile toward collagen. Each fits a different person, and none has been studied for acne in the way whey has been.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea protein is the default we would suggest for most people running a dairy-free trial. Single-ingredient pea isolates such as Naked Pea and NOW Sports Pea Protein list about 24 to 27 g of protein per serving, with roughly 2.0 to 2.5 g of leucine per their labels at the time of writing. That is within reach of whey for muscle building. Pea protein does have a mild earthy taste and a slightly gritty texture in water, and plant proteins carry a higher average heavy metal risk than dairy, so testing matters more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Egg white protein is the closest functional swap for whey. Naked Egg lists about 25 g of protein and about 2.33 g of leucine in a two-scoop serving, per its label. It mixes thinner than whey and has a faint egg taste unflavored. The obvious limit is egg allergy, which is common in children. Egg is not dairy, so it removes milk proteins completely, but it is still an animal protein with its own insulin response, which is smaller than whey's but not zero.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beef protein isolate suits people avoiding both dairy and eggs, or those following a paleo pattern. The catch is that a gram of beef isolate does less for muscle than a gram of whey or egg, so plan on a larger dose if it is your main shake. Our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">beef protein powder guide</a> and <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">egg white protein guide</a> go deeper on each category.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want more plant variety than pea alone, blends of pea with rice, pumpkin seed or other legumes are widely available and are covered in our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein powder guide</a>. Vegetarians who still eat eggs have the widest choice of all; the new <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-vegetarians/">protein powder for vegetarians guide</a> covers that ground.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label-extras" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sugar, Sweeteners and Other Label Extras
          </h2>

          <p>
            The strongest trial evidence in diet and acne is not about protein at all. It is about glycemic load. A 2007 randomized trial in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition put 43 young men with acne on either a low glycemic load diet or a usual diet for 12 weeks; the low glycemic load group had a greater fall in lesion counts. Later trials and the 2022 JAAD International review point in the same direction, though the effects were moderate and the studies small.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes added sugar worth checking on a protein label. Most dedicated protein powders are low in sugar, often 0 to 2 g a serving. Mass gainers, meal replacement shakes and some flavored powders are a different story, carrying large amounts of maltodextrin or cane sugar. If you are trying to give your skin every advantage, a powder with minimal added sugar mixed into water or unsweetened milk alternative is a better bet than a sugary shake blended with juice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweeteners themselves have not been shown to affect acne in either direction, so we did not penalise stevia or sucralose for skin reasons. We did favour unflavored or minimally flavored options for a different reason: fewer ingredients make a self-experiment cleaner. If your skin changes, you want as few variables as possible to explain it.
          </p>

          <p>
            One additive worth knowing about is vitamin B12. Case reports going back decades describe acne-like eruptions after high-dose B12 supplements or injections, and a 2015 study in Science Translational Medicine found that B12 supplementation changed the behavior of the acne-associated skin bacterium in a small group of volunteers. Fortified meal shakes sometimes add B vitamins at many times the daily value. Plain protein powders usually do not, but read the panel. Iodine is also blamed online, but we found too little direct evidence on ordinary dietary amounts to use it in our rankings.
          </p>

