        <div id="elimination" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Run a Fair Elimination Trial
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you suspect your protein shake, the most useful thing you can do is test it properly rather than guess. Acne changes slowly. Skin cells and follicles turn over across weeks, and most dermatologists judge any acne treatment over two to three months, not days. A two-week break from whey that coincides with a good fortnight tells you very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by writing down a baseline. Take photos of your face, chest and back in the same light every week, and note the number of new inflamed spots. Then replace your whey with one alternative, such as an unflavored pea or egg white protein, at the same daily protein amount. Change only that. Keep your skincare, any prescribed treatment, your training and the rest of your diet as steady as you reasonably can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hold the switch for at least eight to twelve weeks. If your skin is clearly better by the end, you have a reasonable personal signal that the whey was contributing. Some people then reintroduce whey for a few weeks to confirm the pattern; if the breakouts come back, the case gets stronger. If nothing changes after three months without dairy protein, the shake was probably not the problem and you can return to whey if you prefer it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not stack experiments. Cutting whey, dairy, sugar and gluten all at once while starting a new cleanser makes any improvement impossible to interpret. And do not drop or delay a treatment your dermatologist has prescribed in order to run a diet test. The two can run side by side; a switch of protein powder is a small, low-risk change that sits comfortably alongside proper treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep an eye on total protein while you experiment. People often cut their shake and forget to replace it, which can undercut training goals. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives a daily target, and every powder in this guide can cover a shake's worth of it without dairy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-triggers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Other Things Gym-Goers Should Rule Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before blaming the protein, consider what else changed when you started training. The most common confounder is sweat and friction. Tight synthetic gym clothes, chest straps, helmets and backpacks trap sweat and rub against skin, and dermatologists recognize a pattern called acne mechanica that appears where pressure and friction are greatest. Showering soon after a session and changing out of damp clothes are cheap fixes worth trying alongside any diet change.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anabolic steroids and other androgens are a well-established cause of acne, often severe and often on the chest and back. Some supplements sold as testosterone boosters or prohormones have been found to contain undeclared steroids, and contamination of sports supplements more broadly is a documented problem. That is one reason we favour products with third-party certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, which screen for banned substances.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age and hormones matter too. Acne peaks in the teenage years, which is also when many people start lifting and buying their first tub of whey. Hormonal shifts in women, including those linked to the menstrual cycle, contraception changes and conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome, can bring breakouts on independently. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-pcos/">protein powder for PCOS guide</a> covers that overlap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some medications can cause or worsen acne, including corticosteroids and certain other drugs. If your breakouts began around the time you started a new prescription, raise it with your doctor rather than stopping it yourself. Stress and poor sleep are often cited as aggravating factors too, though the evidence there is less precise.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this rules whey out. It simply means the shake is one variable among several, and frequently not the most likely one. A short honest list of what changed when your skin got worse is a better starting point than any single supplement swap.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dermatologist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to See a Dermatologist
          </h2>

          <p>
            This guide is about choosing a protein powder. It is not medical advice and it is not a treatment plan. Acne is a medical condition with effective, evidence-based treatments, and no protein powder switch is a substitute for them. If your acne is moderate or severe, leaves dark marks or scars, is painful, or is affecting your confidence, see a dermatologist or your doctor rather than relying on diet changes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seek care sooner if you have deep, painful nodules or cysts, sudden severe acne, acne that started in adulthood with no previous history, or acne alongside other signs such as irregular periods or excess hair growth. Those patterns can point to an underlying hormonal issue that needs proper assessment. Teenagers using protein supplements should involve a parent and, ideally, their doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you go, bring the facts. Tell your clinician what protein supplements and other products you take, when you started them, and what you have already tried. Bring the weekly photos if you have them. Dermatologists are familiar with the whey case reports and will usually be glad to fold a protein switch into a wider plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have a dairy, egg, soy or pea allergy, kidney disease, or are pregnant or breastfeeding, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding or changing a protein supplement. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> covers the general risks, including heavy metals and contamination.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How We Chose These Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We built this list from the label, not from a lab or a taste panel. We have not tested these powders on anyone's skin, and no protein powder has been shown in a trial to reduce acne. What we looked for instead was a set of products that make a clean self-experiment easy: a clear protein source, a short ingredient list, low added sugar and, where possible, independent testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most picks are dairy-free, because removing milk protein is the test that matters most if you suspect whey. We included one plain whey isolate for people who want to try the smallest change first. Within each protein type we preferred unflavored or minimally flavored options, single-source protein, and brands that publish third-party certification such as NSF Contents Certified, NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nutrition figures come from the label or the brand's listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor and batch. Prices were checked at a single retailer on the date given in our label database and will move, so the cost per 25 g of protein figures are approximate. Taste and mixing notes, where they appear, reflect buyer feedback rather than our own testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we did not rank anything on claims of being acne safe, skin friendly or hormone free. No regulator defines those phrases, and we found no product that backs them with acne-specific evidence. A plain pea or egg white powder with a sensible label does everything a skin-focused powder can honestly promise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting results in two weeks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acne develops over weeks, and treatments are judged over two to three months. Dropping whey for a fortnight and calling it a failure, or a success, tells you almost nothing. Commit to at least eight to twelve weeks on the replacement before deciding, and take weekly photos in the same light so you can compare honestly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming isolate removes the problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey isolate strips out lactose and fat, which helps digestion, but if dairy proteins affect your skin at all, the leading hypothesis points to the protein's insulin and IGF-1 effects. Isolate still has those. If you want a real test of whether whey is involved, replace it with pea, egg or beef protein rather than a cleaner whey.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Changing everything at once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dropping whey, cutting sugar, starting a new cleanser and buying a new moisturizer in the same week makes it impossible to know what helped. Change the protein powder first and keep everything else steady. If the skin improves, you know why. If not, you can move on to the next variable with a clear conclusion.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Delaying proper treatment for a diet test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moderate to severe acne can scar, and scarring is permanent. A protein swap is a small experiment, not a treatment. Run it alongside whatever your dermatologist recommends rather than instead of it, and never stop a prescribed medication to see whether diet alone is enough. The two approaches work fine together.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on acne-safe marketing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No regulator defines acne safe, skin friendly or hormone free, and we found no product that backs those claims with acne-specific evidence. A plain pea or egg white powder with a short label and third-party testing does everything a skin-branded powder can honestly offer, usually for less money per gram of protein.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does whey protein cause acne?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not in most people. The link comes from case reports of five or six patients each and one small 60-day observational study, with no controlled trials. Some individuals do seem to break out after starting whey, and the insulin and IGF-1 hypothesis makes that plausible. If your skin worsened after starting whey, a dairy-free trial is reasonable, but whey is not proven to cause acne generally.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for acne?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No powder treats acne. For people who suspect whey, a single-ingredient pea protein such as Naked Pea is our top pick for a dairy-free trial, because it removes milk protein and has nothing else on the label. Naked Egg is the closest match to whey for muscle building. Pick based on what you can eat and will actually drink for three months.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey isolate better than concentrate for acne?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no evidence that it is. Isolate has less lactose and fat, which helps people who are lactose intolerant, but it is still whey protein with a strong insulin response. If you want the smallest possible change, a plain isolate is a reasonable first step for mild acne. For a meaningful test of whey's role, switch to a non-dairy protein instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long should I stop whey to see if it affects my skin?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Plan on at least eight to twelve weeks. Acne changes slowly, and dermatologists typically judge treatments over two to three months. Replace the whey with an equal amount of pea, egg or beef protein, change nothing else, and take weekly photos. If skin clearly improves, consider reintroducing whey briefly to see whether breakouts return before drawing firm conclusions.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is plant protein better for acne-prone skin?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Plant protein removes dairy, which is the main suspect, but there is no direct evidence that plant protein improves acne. It is simply the cheapest and most widely available way to run a dairy-free trial. Egg white and beef isolate remove dairy too. Choose a plant powder low in added sugar, since high glycemic load diets have some trial evidence linking them to acne.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can sugar in protein shakes make acne worse?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Possibly. Small randomized trials found that low glycemic load diets reduced acne lesions compared with usual diets, and reviews support a link between high glycemic load and acne. Most protein powders contain 0 to 2 g of sugar a serving, but gainers, meal shakes and some flavored powders carry much more. Mixing with water rather than juice also helps keep the load down.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I see a dermatologist instead of changing protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  If your acne is moderate, severe, painful, scarring or affecting your wellbeing, yes, and soon. A protein switch is a small experiment, not a treatment, and it can run alongside proper care. Tell your dermatologist which supplements you take and when you started them. This guide is general information about choosing a powder and is not medical advice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
